@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-json-merge-patch-no-dedicated-error-type-shallow-structural-sharing-on-untouched-branches
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Merge Patch: no dedicated error type, shallow structural sharing on untouched branches"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` (RFC 7386) is the third public operation in this module, after
+`resolvePointer`/`JsonPointerError` (RFC 6901, dl-001) and `applyPatch`/`JsonPatchError` (RFC 6902,
+dl-002). Both of those throw a dedicated error class for malformed input. RFC 7386 is structurally
+different from RFC 6902: it has no operation vocabulary, no path syntax, and no notion of an operation
+"failing to apply" — §2 of the RFC defines a total function from any two JSON values (`target`,
+`patch`) to a result, with no case left undefined.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch` does not throw, and no `JsonMergePatchError` class is exported. The precedent set
+  by dl-001/dl-002 ("one error class per public operation") was for operations that the RFC itself
+  describes as capable of being invalid or failing; RFC 7386 describes no such case for `applyMergePatch`
+  itself — every JSON value is a valid merge patch, and every JSON value is a valid target. Adding an
+  error class with no way to trigger it would be speculative, which the code-quality directive already
+  discourages elsewhere in this module (dl-001 made the same call about splitting `JsonPointerError` by
+  cause).
+- Object members of `target` that the `patch` object does not mention are carried over into the result
+  by reference, not deep-cloned, at every level of the recursion. Each recursive step only shallow-copies
+  the object it is currently merging into (`{ ...target }`) before deleting or overwriting the members
+  the patch actually touches. This is the same copy-on-write invariant dl-002 relied on for `applyPatch`'s
+  `copy` operation: nothing in this module ever mutates a node in place, so sharing an untouched subtree
+  between `target` and the result cannot let a later write to one leak into the other.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers cannot distinguish "nothing to apply" from "malformed patch" because the second case does not
+  exist for this function; passing it any value that round-trips through `JSON.parse`/`JSON.stringify`
+  (including `undefined`-free arrays, objects, scalars, and `null`) always returns a result, never throws.
+  A caller that wants to reject a patch shape for business reasons (e.g. forbidding `null` at the top
+  level) must do so itself before calling `applyMergePatch`.
+- As with `copy` in `dl-002`, this is only safe as long as every write this module performs stays
+  copy-on-write. Any future change to `applyMergePatch` (or a shared helper it starts using) that would
+  mutate an object in place instead of shallow-copying it first would silently corrupt `target` through
+  the shared references this decision relies on.
+- A merge patch that targets a value identical in content to what is already there, or that touches no
+  members at all (e.g. `{}`), still allocates a new shallow copy of every object on the path from the
+  root to the touched member, even where the content doesn't change. This is the same cost profile
+  `applyPatch` already has for every write; no new tradeoff is introduced here.
