@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-applymergepatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch RFC 7386 applyMergePatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing RFC 7386 JSON
+Merge Patch:
+
+- Follows the RFC's own pseudocode (§2) directly: if `patch` is a JSON object, start from `target` if
+  it is also an object (otherwise from `{}`), then for each member of `patch` either delete the
+  corresponding member of the result (value `null`) or recursively merge-patch it in (any other
+  value); if `patch` is not an object, it replaces the target outright (this also covers `patch` being
+  an array, `null`, or a scalar).
+- Reuses the existing `isJsonObject` helper from the RFC 6901/6902 implementation already in this
+  module, rather than re-deriving the object-vs-other-JSON-value test.
+- Immutable by construction: each recursive call that descends into an object member builds a new
+  shallow copy (`{ ...target }`) before mutating that copy with `delete`/assignment, so `target` (and
+  every object nested in it) is never mutated. Members the patch doesn't touch keep their original
+  object reference (structural sharing), matching the copy-on-write convention `dl-002` established
+  for `applyPatch`.
+- Unlike `applyPatch`, `applyMergePatch` never throws: RFC 7386 assigns a defined merge result to
+  every possible JSON value as a patch, so there is no "invalid merge patch" to reject. See `dl-003`
+  for why no `JsonMergePatchError` class was added.
+
+Test-first: added the full RFC 7386 Appendix A example table (member replace/add, `null`-removes-member
+including removal from a target that has other members, non-object patch replacing a differently-shaped
+value, nested-object merge with a `null` removing a nested member, array values replaced wholesale, a
+top-level array/scalar/`null` patch replacing the whole document, and removing a member down to an
+empty nested object) to `src/index.test.ts`, confirmed red against the missing `applyMergePatch` export,
+then implemented. Added further edge-path tests beyond the RFC's table: a `null` for a member absent
+from the target is a no-op (not an addition), an object patch against a non-object/array/`null` target
+starts from `{}`, an empty object patch is a no-op, and a non-mutation check against a deep snapshot of
+a target with nested objects touched by both a `null` removal and a recursive merge.
+
+Documented `applyMergePatch` in `README.md` under "API", alongside `resolvePointer` and `applyPatch`.
+
+Verified `src/index.ts` type-checks cleanly under the project's strict `tsconfig.json`, using the same
+one-off temporary `typescript` install approach as task-001/task-002 (copied `index.ts` alone into a
+scratch directory with an equivalent `tsconfig.json`, since the project's own `tsconfig.json` also
+includes `src/index.test.ts`, which fails to type-check for the pre-existing reasons already on record:
+missing `@types/node` for `node:test`/`node:assert`, and the `.ts`-extension-in-relative-imports
+convention needed for `node --test`'s native type stripping not being reconciled with `tsc`/NodeNext's
+`.js`-extension convention — neither is introduced or worsened by this change).
+
+`npm test` passes (60/60, up from the pre-existing 47).
+
+## Follow-ups (not done here, out of scope for this task)
+
+- Same pre-existing gaps noted in task-001/task-002/task-003: no `typescript`/`@types/node`
+  devDependencies or `typecheck` script wired into `package.json`.
